<h2>The Complete Guide to the 12 Seater Maharaja Tempo Traveller in Bangalore</h2>
<p>The 12 seater Maharaja Tempo Traveller is for groups that want space more than they want a seat count. It carries 12 passengers in wide, sofa-style seating with curtains, push-back comfort, reading lights and charging points, at a per-km rate far below any luxury van. This guide explains what "Maharaja" means in practice, who the vehicle suits, how to seat and pack a group of 12, what real trips cost, and how it compares with the 12 seater Urbania and the other vehicles in the fleet. All rates are the live Yogi Tours &amp; Travels tariff: ₹22 per km with AC and ₹20 per km non-AC, a 300 km daily minimum and ₹500 per day driver Bata, with tolls, parking, permits and state taxes billed at actuals.</p>

<h2>Part 1. What "Maharaja" Means</h2>

<h3>Fewer seats, more room</h3>
<p>A standard 12 seater Tempo Traveller packs a row of narrow seats tightly. The Maharaja configuration trades some of that density for width and legroom per passenger, laid out in a lounge-style arrangement. The result is a cabin where people sit more like guests in a living room than commuters on a bench. For a family group on a six-hour drive, that is the difference between arriving tired and arriving comfortable.</p>
<p>Our Maharaja Tempo Traveller has wide sofa-style seating, curtains for the windows, push-back seats, reading lights, charging points and a luggage area. It is air-conditioned on the AC rate, and a non-AC rate is available for short or cool-weather trips.</p>

<h3>What it is not</h3>
<p>The Maharaja Tempo Traveller is not an Urbania. It uses the traditional Traveller van body, so the ride is that of a sturdy commercial van, not a car-like monocoque. It does not have the Urbania's leather recliners, Smart TV or on-board chiller. What it offers instead is real comfort at ₹22 per km, against ₹38 for the <a href="/fleet/tempo-traveller/urbania-12-seater-maharaja">12 seater Maharaja Urbania</a>.</p>

